 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "Direct and indirect objects",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "In \"the master gives the slave food\", the food is what is given: the <b>direct object</b>, in the accusative. The slave is the person it is given to: the <b>indirect object</b>, in the dative."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "dominus <u class=\"c\">servo</u> <u class=\"v\">cibum</u> dat.",
      "en": "The master gives <b>food</b> <b>to the slave</b>.",
      "note": "<em>cibum</em> is what is given; <em>servo</em> is who gets it."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "The indirect object is the person something is given, brought or owed to.",
      "Translate the dative with \"to\" or \"for\": \"gives food to the slave\". English can also leave the word out: \"gives the slave food\".",
      "A dative can also say who something is done for: <em>cenam dominae parat</em>, \"she prepares dinner for the mistress\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
