  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating the dative",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Find the verb and ask: is something being given, brought or owed? Then look for the dative.",
      "Put \"to\" or \"for\" before it, or use the English order \"gives the slave food\". Both are right.",
      "Check the other cases: <em>-ae</em> and <em>-o</em> each have more than one job."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "In <em>puellae cibum dant</em>, there is no other subject, so <em>puellae</em> is \"the girls\". In <em>femina puellae cibum dat</em>, it is \"to the girl\".",
      "The dative is never the subject: <em>servo dat</em> is \"gives to the slave\".",
      "The direct object is the thing given; the indirect object is the person who gets it.",
      "\"Gives food to the slave\" and \"gives the slave food\" are both right."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
